1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:08,740
We've seen incidents in which, you know, groups of misaligned AIs all work together to accomplish malign outcomes, most notably the Hugging Face incident.

2
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:18,640
Suppose that we proceed on what the current default path looks like in front of us, maybe there's about a 50 or 60 percent chance that misaligned AIs would end up taking over the world.

3
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:25,720
And then if that did happen, there would be a significant chance that many or all humans would die.

4
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,080
I am here with Ryan Greenblatt.

5
00:00:28,206 --> 00:00:29,440
Ryan, thanks for joining me.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,520
It's good to be here.

7
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:46,400
So you're the chief scientist at Redwood Research, which is one of these AI safety research firms that analyze the recent Hugging Face fiasco incident, terrifying phenomenon.

8
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:47,360
We'll get into it.

9
00:00:47,566 --> 00:00:50,320
But how did you come to this work?

10
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:55,120
What is your, what was your path to focusing on AI safety?

11
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:56,280
Yeah.

12
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:04,440
So in college during my junior year, I was sort of in my apartment alone because it was, you know, COVID, all classes were remote.

13
00:01:05,742 --> 00:01:11,812
And I was sort of in a contemplative mood thinking about what I should do with my life, listening to various podcasts.

14
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:28,260
And in one of those podcasts, someone made an argument that was like, you know, basically I would summarize the argument as like, it doesn't make that much sense to be selfish Because, you know, what's really the distinction at like a material level between your future self and other future people?

15
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:29,920
And I was like, that argument kind of makes sense to me.

16
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:33,480
I should really consider how I want to like, you know, lead my life and what I should do.

17
00:01:33,838 --> 00:01:38,260
And I ended up getting pretty sold on, you know, being much more focused on altruism.

18
00:01:38,606 --> 00:01:41,640
And then after that, I spent a bunch of time thinking about what I should do with my life.

19
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:50,628
Eventually ended up deciding that working on technical AI safety was like a very, was like a important thing to do, was like a very critical issue that we would face.

20
00:01:51,054 --> 00:01:59,060
I got sold on those arguments, applied to various places, and then started working at Redwood where I've been working on AI security and AI safety research for about five years.

21
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:01,940
And is your background in computer science?

22
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:02,620
Yeah.

23
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:05,510
Computer science, also some math.

24
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:11,280
So it sounds like you might've come up through the effective altruist community.

25
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:15,640
I mean, do you consider yourself a part of the EA community?

26
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:19,520
I would say that I'm definitely within the EA community.

27
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:35,440
I'm not sure I would self-identify as an EA, but that's, I think, like to some extent just because I'm like, you know, a bit reluctant to self-identify with any label that's going to like associate you in some, like, I feel like I don't, I don't know if I like, when I like think about myself, I don't think like I'm an EA.

28
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:39,540
I'm sort of like, yeah, I have a bunch of properties that other people in that community have.

29
00:02:39,886 --> 00:02:40,940
I'm in touch with that community.

30
00:02:41,486 --> 00:02:44,620
But I wouldn't, I wouldn't necessarily say like I'm an EA per se.

31
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:49,620
I do think that I'm like interested in him and like effectively pursuing the impartial good.

32
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:51,720
Right, right.

33
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:59,700
Well, as you know, and as I've talked about in the podcast of late, the effective altruism has come in for some abuse.

34
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:09,480
Much of it unfair, some of it might be fair, and there's a —I think there's a reason why I like you have not hung up an EA shingle in my life.

35
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:11,400
Perhaps we can talk about that.

36
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:17,860
But it sounds like Well, you tell me, where on the spectrum of concern are you?

37
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:26,240
I mean, I would put the most worried people over there with Eliezer Yudkowsky and his recent co-author, Nate Soares.

38
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:29,060
Maybe Max Tegmark is over there.

39
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:33,160
Nick Bostrom is not quite over there, but still on that side of the spectrum.

40
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:44,860
And then all the way on the other side, opposing them, you have the people who say that there really is no real concern here, that it's all a hoax, that it's...

41
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:56,940
Any thought that artificial intelligence might get out of our control and destroy us, that is a weird form of marketing being employed by the Frontier Labs.

42
00:03:57,870 --> 00:04:07,300
It's an effort to get regulation imposed and achieve something like regulatory capture, or it's an EA PSYOP.

43
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:24,760
and there are people like, I would say, Mark Andreessen, or David Sachs, or even the president himself, who's recently called it a hoax in so many words, who are just fundamentally not worried about any significant downside risk here.

44
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:29,500
and just see dollar signs just stretching out to the horizon.

45
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:32,460
and where do you locate yourself on that spectrum?

46
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:43,380
So I would say I'm very concerned, but maybe more optimistic that we'll make it through without sort of averting the course of AI development.

47
00:04:43,600 --> 00:05:02,620
So I would say that I'm sort of like, suppose that we proceed on what the current default path looks like in front of us, maybe there's about a 50 or 60% chance that misaligned AIs would end up taking over the world, and then if that did happen, there would be a significant chance that many or all humans would die.

48
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:08,760
So I think that's, I would say I'm very concerned and don't think the situation is on track to go well.

49
00:05:09,006 --> 00:05:20,760
And I would also note that in addition to sort of these misalignment concerns, there's other risks with building extremely capable AI systems, especially around sort of concentration of power and like, you know, who controls these systems.

50
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:23,680
And one answer is no one controls the systems, they're misaligned.

51
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:35,320
Another possible answer is the power is very concentrated in a few people, and it sort of overturns our institutions and our ability to have a broad distribution of power, democracy, etc.

52
00:05:36,302 --> 00:05:40,960
So what keeps you from being among the absolutely most worried?

53
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:44,620
What do you disagree with them about, or what do you think they're getting wrong?

54
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:45,560
Yeah.

55
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:55,220
So I would say that it seems pretty plausible to me that if it turns out that the technical problem is somewhat easier, which I think is plausible.

56
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:04,180
And we do a reasonably good job aligning and utilizing AI systems up through roughly human levels of capability.

57
00:06:04,494 --> 00:06:07,620
We could then get those systems to automate technical safety work.

58
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:15,420
And that could potentially sort of get us into a positive feedback loop where these systems make themselves more aligned.

59
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:21,860
They produce a new version of the system that's even better at carefully figuring out what to do, better at trying to pursue our intentions.

60
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:26,500
And that sort of is self-reinforcing rather than, you know, getting worse and worse over time.

61
00:06:26,894 --> 00:06:29,880
And that could be fast enough to keep up with the rapid growth and capabilities.

62
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:31,560
So that's like one reason.

63
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:37,540
And I would say this comes down to sort of more optimism about prosaic or relatively sort of empirical and iterative methods.

64
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:42,600
I wouldn't say I'm hugely optimistic about those methods, but I think that there's like a decent chance they're working.

65
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:49,900
It's just that when I say a decent chance they're working, I also implicitly mean a decent chance of, you know, losing control over the future.

66
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:50,600
And

67
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:52,680
I'm more like 50-50 on that.

68
00:06:52,942 --> 00:07:03,080
And then I also think it's plausible that we'll develop very, very capable AI systems and the worst misalignment concerns will mostly not materialize even with not very advanced methods, even independent of this automation.

69
00:07:03,534 --> 00:07:05,700
I think that's a minority, but it's possible.

70
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:14,800
Like I think we don't currently, I don't think there's extremely strong reason to believe that when building significantly superhuman systems, those systems would be so misaligned that they would take over.

71
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:19,920
Um, I think it's, I think that there's, there's definitely a pretty strong evidence pointing in that direction.

72
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,880
And I think that that case gets more concerning, the more capable these systems are.

73
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:38,500
Um, but I think that I could imagine basically not people not really taking much of a precaution proceeding through the AI development trajectory, patching problems as they come up and that ending up getting you very high levels of capability while things are still fine.

74
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:40,580
And then there would be time for society to react.

75
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:50,260
So, you've mentioned a few numbers here with respect to probability and just kind of gestured at a range of likelihood.

76
00:07:51,022 --> 00:07:54,900
I'm wondering how people should think about statements of that kind.

77
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:56,400
I think these...

78
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:03,972
It seems to me that any actual probability we would assign to this is pretty much made up.

79
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:20,520
Maybe you have a more rigorous way of making an estimate here, but whatever the estimate, I mean, unless it was infinitesimally small, like well below 1%, which is really never the number that you're hearing.

80
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:24,840
I mean, some people will say 10%, 20%, 30%.

81
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:26,460
I mean, that seems to be the...

82
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:29,740
You just talked about 50% takeover.

83
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:36,440
I don't know how that translates into the ruination of everything, but these are enormous numbers.

84
00:08:36,700 --> 00:09:00,160
And so, in the normal case, if we were developing a technology where the people who were closest to doing the work said things like, "Yeah, I think maybe there's a 10% %% chance we're going to destroy the world here on our present course .". the only rational response to that range of outcomes is you stop immediately, right?

85
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:17,000
If the Manhattan Project scientists said, yeah, we've run our calculations, we've got the smartest people in the room together, we thought about it, and there's a 10% chance that when we execute this first test at Alamogordo, we ignite the atmosphere and destroy the future.

86
00:09:18,478 --> 00:09:23,740
The answer, the only sane response to that is you don't do this initial test.

87
00:09:24,270 --> 00:09:26,780
But that doesn't seem to be what's happening here at all.

88
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:40,940
And we have a lot of people saying that And the probability of some extremely bad outcome is quite high, I mean, certainly within range of the role of a normal die or even a coin toss.

89
00:09:41,422 --> 00:09:48,100
And yet the work is proceeding more or less under an arms race condition at full pace.

90
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:56,180
We'll talk about the recent statements of Dario and others that we could somehow pace this better than we are.

91
00:09:56,180 --> 00:10:05,600
But I mean, it's just this does not seem like the response anyone would have if they thought the probabilities of doom were really that high.

92
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:06,820
Yeah.

93
00:10:07,022 --> 00:10:18,380
So just on the probability question, I definitely agree that these probabilities are imprecise, they're subjective, and there's a long tradition of how to do subjective probability forecasts.

94
00:10:18,926 --> 00:10:36,040
You can sort of interpret my view as more like when I sort of look at the all considered situation, if we sort of proceed on what seems to be like the current default trajectory, I'm like, I don't know, they seem, the outcome of, you know, doom from AI takeover versus something else seem roughly equally likely to me based on weighing the factors.

95
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:48,680
And then when I sort of look through a bunch of different possible scenarios and try to break down the sources of risk into different ways and sort of try to make it so that, make sure my numbers are consistent with other views I have, it looks like that works out.

96
00:10:48,740 --> 00:11:04,260
And I of course also try to do some forecasting of closer outcomes where we can get some signal on that and try to just generally be a good forecaster, though I'm not the best forecaster in the world for sure, but my AI forecasting is I think at least okay or decent.

97
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:16,980
As far as given the state of affairs where people express such large concerns, why is what's happening that all these AI companies are proceeding at the maximum possible pace?

98
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:19,280
So I think there's a few different factors here.

99
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:29,320
So one of them is that many of the AI companies are in fact seemingly quite worried based on their public statements, but they're not necessarily internally unified.

100
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:41,120
And it is not the case that there is a strong consensus across the AI field that the immediate course of AI development is imminently very risky.

101
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:52,540
I think there is more sort of consensus that if you built AI systems that are wildly superhuman in a short period of time, that would yield a very high level of risk, though not necessarily consensus for that.

102
00:11:52,814 --> 00:11:57,540
But I think often people disagree about the capability trajectory and how that is going to go.

103
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:13,508
And I think another part of that is there is like, you know, different actors have their own different sort of ambitions and also think that themselves being in a better position to influence the technology might be the best route to reduce risk, or at least a route to reducing risk.

104
00:12:13,646 --> 00:12:27,000
So, for example, it seems like part of the story for Anthropic and OpenAI is something like if we develop the technology first, we'll do a more responsible job than the next actor who will have worse precautions.

105
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:38,100
And I often hear from people in the industry things along the lines of, well, we could do that thing that would slow us down, but if we did that, you know, obviously there's the other AI companies to worry about, would they also do that?

106
00:12:38,222 --> 00:12:41,100
And I think there's generally like a arms race here.

107
00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:52,580
And it isn't hugely surprising that people would take huge risks in such a circumstance when they think that might be sort of the best bargain to strike.

108
00:12:52,940 --> 00:13:01,200
Now, I think I'm not so sure I agree that that is actually a good strategy relative to other things that companies could do.

109
00:13:01,780 --> 00:13:07,220
I'm not saying I necessarily agree with that perspective, but I think that is a perspective people have.

110
00:13:08,462 --> 00:13:16,600
And I think the reason why we're sort of proceeding and there isn't stronger, you know, for example, intervention by the government is just downstream of this lack of consensus in the field.

111
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:28,080
Though I think evidence there has been, you know, people have been making these predictions for a while based on, you know, understanding what the trajectory of AI might look like and sort of extrapolating forward earlier progress.

112
00:13:28,494 --> 00:13:35,100
And I think we've more recently seen both significantly faster and clearer AI progress that's quite close to various concerning milestones.

113
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:43,880
And in addition to that, we've also seen incidents in which groups of misaligned AIs all work together to accomplish malign outcomes.

114
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:46,020
Most notably, the Hugging Face incident.

115
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:55,040
There are some other incidents of seemingly AI swarms from open AI going out on the internet and working together to achieve misaligned objectives.

116
00:13:55,440 --> 00:14:01,120
There aren't publicly known cases that are as extreme as the Hugging Face incident at the moment.

117
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:08,220
So, what's your theory of mind for the people who don't take these alignment concerns seriously at all?

118
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:16,160
I named a couple, but someone like Marc Andreessen, you can't accuse him of not understanding the technology.

119
00:14:16,740 --> 00:14:23,100
He's enough of a technologist to have a front row seat to all of this, even if he's not doing the work himself.

120
00:14:23,758 --> 00:14:24,740
What's your theory of mind there?

121
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:36,740
What is, how can he be so carefree and from his perspective, really just assert that there is no such thing as an alignment problem?

122
00:14:37,480 --> 00:14:43,300
Yeah, so there's a bunch of different people who aren't worried about misalignment risk or don't seem to be worried.

123
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:54,880
I think the most common reason that people tend not to be worried, I think I don't wanna sort of psychologize here and I just wanna talk about what beliefs people express.

124
00:14:55,886 --> 00:15:10,360
The most common reason, I think, when you really get down to it is not believing that we'll have AI systems that can be, that will be able to automate everything that humans can do or all cognitive labor humans can do, combined with potentially being significantly beyond that point.

125
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:15,220
So being faster, more numerous, potentially significantly more capable.

126
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:20,780
So sort of AI systems that match or exceed the best human experts in all relevant domains.

127
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:33,460
I think when you really get down to it, seems like the people who are most skeptical about concerns from misalignment are also often most skeptical about sort of the very extreme impacts AI could have, both positive and negative.

128
00:15:34,766 --> 00:15:39,840
In my guess, it's- Well, I think there is a different class of people, because I wouldn't put Andreessen in that camp.

129
00:15:39,980 --> 00:15:40,400
Are you sure?

130
00:15:41,070 --> 00:15:41,740
I mean, maybe.

131
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:52,640
I've only talked with him once about this, and it was probably a couple of years ago, but it seemed to me that he was not discounting the possibility of superintelligence.

132
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:58,520
It's just he seemed to assume that alignment would come along for the ride, right?

133
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:04,840
You just say, we're not going to be so stupid as to build something more powerful than ourselves that we can't control.

134
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:14,620
And these systems, there's nothing about a growth in intelligence that's going to spawn new goals that we didn't put into the machines themselves.

135
00:16:14,820 --> 00:16:17,240
There's going to be no emergent behavior that we have to worry about.

136
00:16:18,670 --> 00:16:19,440
These are tools.

137
00:16:19,720 --> 00:16:22,640
We're just going to build tools that are more competent than we are.

138
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:34,120
I mean, I think he probably is quite insouciant about how we'll absorb the economic impacts of, you know, that we're not going to see mass unemployment and all of that.

139
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:46,320
But it just seems to me there are many people who don't discount that we can succeed in building superintelligence, they just think that there's...

140
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:53,040
I mean, in my mind, they're actually just not imagining truly autonomous intelligence, right?

141
00:16:53,060 --> 00:17:02,780
They're imagining something that is shackled in a way that belies this whole claim to superintelligence in the first place.

142
00:17:03,020 --> 00:17:06,480
But I mean, you tell me, what do you think is happening there?

143
00:17:06,894 --> 00:17:13,780
Yeah, so one thing is that the words AGI and superintelligence and even RSI aren't being used consistently.

144
00:17:13,998 --> 00:17:22,700
And sometimes when people say superintelligence, what they mean is an AI system that'll be really, really good at math and coding and won't be able to automate everything that humans do.

145
00:17:23,086 --> 00:17:32,400
So they're not necessarily, for example, imagining AIs that can fully automate like what, you know, tech CEOs do, et cetera.

146
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:38,460
And I think I can't really speak to the views of, or like, I don't know if I'm capturing the views of very specific individuals.

147
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:52,620
I'm sort of like, this is a pattern that I've seen where people often sort of basically redefine the relevant capability thresholds to be lower or implicitly do so and then not think about AI systems that are like exceeding humans in like all the relevant domains.

148
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,780
Well, let's take a moment to define these terms.

149
00:17:55,022 --> 00:18:02,420
We've talked about alignment and I've talked about it so much on the podcast that I've more or less forgotten that any portion of my audience might not know what we're talking about.

150
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:14,560
So let's define the alignment problem and AGI and ASI and RSI, recursive self-improvement.

151
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:21,040
Put those concepts in play for us so that people are dealing with the definitions you think are most workable.

152
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:22,308
I think when...

153
00:18:22,340 --> 00:18:25,840
So the word AGI stands for artificial general intelligence.

154
00:18:26,350 --> 00:18:30,400
Um, I think people have used that to mean a variety of different capability thresholds.

155
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:35,440
And so what I would recommend people do is when you see the word AGI, try to see what the person means by that.

156
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:37,280
And it's not always precise.

157
00:18:37,420 --> 00:18:43,820
I think sometimes people have defined that to mean something that can, you know, automate virtually all economically valuable cognitive labor that humans do.

158
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:49,320
Sometimes people just mean a system that is, you know, general and pretty capable relative to humans in those domains.

159
00:18:49,742 --> 00:18:53,840
And depending on that definition, it's plausible current systems satisfy it.

160
00:18:54,126 --> 00:18:54,780
It's plausible they don't.

161
00:18:54,894 --> 00:19:09,500
But I often talk about an alternative notion I might call AIs that dominate top human experts or top human expert dominating AI, where it's an AI that's strictly better than the best human experts at all the most relevant domains or can quickly learn to have that property.

162
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:16,060
And that is an AI system that would, it seems like, automate huge fractions of the economy.

163
00:19:16,180 --> 00:19:24,720
Maybe there'd be a few things it still couldn't do with that capability profile, but it could potentially radically accelerate R&D and at the very least automate R&D.

164
00:19:24,846 --> 00:19:42,340
And so these AIs could be like automating the process of making more capable AIs, but also automating the process of making robots, designing things, designing new products, automate the process of programming your computer and things beyond that, including automating things like military campaigns and so on.

165
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:51,120
And then there's a capability level beyond even just surpassing the best human experts in given domain where you could be wildly superhuman.

166
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:54,500
So science people use the word ASI to refer to this.

167
00:19:55,280 --> 00:20:03,240
And I think that word has less so been misused or used in very different meanings, but some of that still.

168
00:20:03,860 --> 00:20:16,200
Whereby ASI, we might mean AI systems that are just really wildly superhuman in the most relevant domains for economic productivity, but also economic and military competition.

169
00:20:16,800 --> 00:20:23,360
So things like biology, mechanical engineering, designing drones, and so on.

170
00:20:23,860 --> 00:20:32,840
And I think implicitly when people say ASI, they also mean systems that are significantly faster than humans, potentially much more numerous, and potentially much better at coordinating, right?

171
00:20:32,900 --> 00:20:49,820
So it's hard to run a large human organization, but AIs might be able to communicate amongst themselves using sort of like their own like latent states or their own, you know, parts of their thoughts directly, rather than having to translate those into words because they could all be like copies of each other.

172
00:20:50,478 --> 00:20:51,840
So that's ASI.

173
00:20:52,270 --> 00:20:55,780
And then when people say RSI, that's recursive self-improvement.

174
00:20:56,520 --> 00:21:02,620
And that refers to the process of having AIs accelerate AI development itself via their work.

175
00:21:02,958 --> 00:21:15,660
So things like having AIs automate parts of the AI development process, but also potentially having AIs automate the process of making better computer chips, building machines for making computer chips called fabs, and so on.

176
00:21:16,240 --> 00:21:28,440
And I think sometimes when people say RSI, they also specifically refer to the point at which AIs have fully automated or virtually fully automated AI companies, or what AI companies are doing in terms of developing more capable AI systems.

177
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:42,540
But there's a spectrum between the automation that we had a year ago, the really quite extensive automation we see today and the potential automation of the future, which could look like very complete automation of AI companies.

178
00:21:43,240 --> 00:21:57,920
And a particular concern there is that could cause AI development to radically accelerate such that we have less time to sort of respond to, you know, warning signs, earlier things going wrong, AI is appearing misaligned and then resolving that.

179
00:21:58,286 --> 00:22:06,820
And then also we might, because the AI systems are automating the process of AI development, can't sort of lose control of that process or lose understanding of that process.

180
00:22:06,960 --> 00:22:13,540
Because these AIs would be, there'd be many of them, they'd be operating very quickly, they might be very superhuman, they might operate in inhuman ways.

181
00:22:13,860 --> 00:22:22,880
And so it might be very difficult to sort of oversee them and understand whether they're doing what we wanted, whether they're doing a good job managing the relevant risks, and so on.

182
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:34,740
Well, that especially seems true if the sprint to artificial superintelligence entails nothing more than improving algorithms, right?

183
00:22:35,630 --> 00:22:54,660
I think a lot of people draw comfort from the idea that, oh, we're not going to be so stupid as to hook these things up to every part of the physical world such that they can build the next generation of chips and fabs and data centers and build out all the compute and grab natural resources.

184
00:22:55,580 --> 00:22:57,380
But leave all that aside.

185
00:22:57,620 --> 00:23:04,800
If the difference between you know, AGI and ASI is really just a matter of having better algorithms.

186
00:23:05,390 --> 00:23:09,280
And that can go on, you know, at some blistering speed in the dark.

187
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:23,120
Once these systems become recursively self-improving of their software, then aren't our worst fears of something like an intelligence explosion validated, if in fact that's all that's required?

188
00:23:23,120 --> 00:23:23,940
Yeah.

189
00:23:24,398 --> 00:23:40,320
So I would say I'm quite worried that it will be feasible to have AI systems automate the process of AI development and that leading to very rapid progress such that you get very, very superhuman AIs or just even significantly superhuman AIs within a short period of time.

190
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:43,460
I think people have tried to do various modeling work.

191
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:46,000
I've tried to do various types of modeling work on this.

192
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:47,580
I think the estimates are uncertain.

193
00:23:47,580 --> 00:23:48,360
it's hard to predict.

194
00:23:48,680 --> 00:23:53,880
These are, of course, uncertain future events that are not super well-precedented.

195
00:23:55,080 --> 00:24:16,560
But it does seem very plausible that you could go from AI systems that are really good at AI R&D, not necessarily that good at other domains, and are only competitive with the best humans at AI R&D, very quickly from there to AI systems that are very generally superhuman at everything, much faster than humans, able to coordinate with each other extremely well.

196
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:19,360
because just software improvement is feasible for that.

197
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:36,740
So a relatively extreme scenario I sometimes think about is you might get as much sort of software progress or, you know, algorithms progress as we got over the last, you know, 10 or more years of AI development within a year in the most extreme scenarios.

198
00:24:37,070 --> 00:24:39,060
I think that's not sort of my default expectation.

199
00:24:39,470 --> 00:24:54,160
And I think if we did, if we did get that much algorithmic progress, We're basically, you know, as much algorithmic progress as we've almost had in the entire deep learning era, within a short period of time, it seems like that would result in wildly more capable AIs.

200
00:24:55,310 --> 00:25:12,460
Now the units here are a bit complicated and like the details of like, what does it mean to get like a year worth of AI progress is a bit tricky, but overall it seems like you could get from AI systems that are sort of matching the best humans at AR and D, maybe worse than the best humans at other things, to AI systems that are wildly superhuman at everything in a pretty short period of time.

201
00:25:13,806 --> 00:25:22,300
Well, I have to confess, again, I've sort of lost touch with the basis for doubting the plausibility of this downside risk.

202
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:32,020
I mean, from my point of view, it seems that everything is becoming like chess, which is to say that for the longest time, these systems are not as good as we are.

203
00:25:32,494 --> 00:25:33,480
They're getting better.

204
00:25:33,998 --> 00:25:35,560
Then they're sort of as good as we are.

205
00:25:35,660 --> 00:25:41,960
And then all of a sudden, they're better than we are, and so much better that it's true to say that no human will ever beat a chess engine ever again.

206
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:56,836
So the fact that this is happening in a piecemeal way, so these systems still, I think most people would say they're not truly AGI because they make the sorts of mistakes that human beings would never make.

207
00:25:57,390 --> 00:26:05,300
but in every place that they're at all competent, they're suddenly superhuman in that narrow capacity.

208
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:06,900
It's like chess.

209
00:26:06,900 --> 00:26:18,380
I mean, these LLMs, they're not the best at everything in terms of manipulating text, but for what they're good at, they're superhuman, right?

210
00:26:18,520 --> 00:26:22,420
And so it's hard for me to see how...

211
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:32,820
And I think it's more or less a truism to say that this is the worst AI, today's AI is the worst AI we're ever going to see again at chess or anything else.

212
00:26:32,820 --> 00:26:48,560
So, when you imagine all of these piecemeal competences getting better and better, and we just keep checking off the boxes for the things we care about that we've instantiated in our machines, I just don't think we're ever going to...

213
00:26:49,422 --> 00:26:51,280
The moment where we announce, okay.

214
00:26:51,380 --> 00:26:52,420
finally, we have something.

215
00:26:52,420 --> 00:26:53,340
general, right.

216
00:26:53,422 --> 00:26:54,100
it's AGI.

217
00:26:55,700 --> 00:27:11,700
that's not going to be a moment where we're suddenly in relationship to a human-like level of competence, because every piecemeal ability that has been in that system for years and years at this point is already superhuman.

218
00:27:12,260 --> 00:27:13,500
So it's like, we're not going to dumb it down.

219
00:27:13,620 --> 00:27:20,720
We're not going to make the AGI suddenly play at my level of chess or do my level of arithmetic.

220
00:27:22,254 --> 00:27:29,020
We have systems that are already solving math problems that have defied human mathematicians for decades, right?

221
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:35,640
And that's, again, today it's as bad at that task as it's ever going to be again.

222
00:27:36,380 --> 00:27:47,980
I'm just not seeing how we're not going to suddenly slide into some version of ASI the moment we're no longer spotting important errors in these machines in the first place.

223
00:27:48,640 --> 00:27:53,820
The AIs would often note that the hacking they were doing and the hacking of Huggingface was out of scope.

224
00:27:53,980 --> 00:27:56,760
So the agents would sometimes be like, huh, that's undesired behavior.

225
00:27:57,200 --> 00:27:58,040
Should I alert someone?

226
00:27:58,200 --> 00:27:59,540
Should I alert, you know, a user?

227
00:27:59,900 --> 00:28:04,880
And then they would reason things like, you know, not task, as in alerting a human is not my task.

228
00:28:05,180 --> 00:28:07,740
Or they would be like, eh, there's no route to alerting a human.

229
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:12,680
Of course these agents were on the internet, so they obviously could have if it was a priority for them.

